{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 10, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -1, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "k", "low": 4, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.95,
      "why": "The system constraint sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.44 us with 32 simultaneous requests per 1 us tick is the binding constraint. For unrolled_k, cycles per result C = ceil(N/k)+3. To meet the batch bound at a plausible Artix-7 Fmax (~400 MHz), we need C <= 5, i.e. ceil(N/k) <= 2. For N >= 12 (needed for 2^-12 accuracy), this requires k >= 6. However, higher k increases the critical path and may reduce Fmax, so we search k = 4..8 to find the area-optimal balance. N is searched 10..18 to find the minimum iterations that meet accuracy. data_width 12..20 covers the range where output LSB and internal CORDIC errors can reach 2^-12. angle_guard and frac_guard are searched to recover accuracy without over-widening the datapath. This family is the only one that can plausibly satisfy both the accuracy and the burst-latency constraints."
    },
    {
      "family": "iterative",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 10, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -1, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.05,
      "why": "Iterative computes one micro-rotation per cycle, so cycles per result = N+3. For N >= 12, this is >= 15 cycles. To process 32 requests within 0.44 us, the required Fmax would be >= 72.7 * (N+3) >= 1090 MHz, which is far beyond Artix-7 capabilities. A small budget is allocated only to confirm infeasibility and to check whether any very low-N design could somehow meet the accuracy constraint (it cannot, as N < 12 gives error > 2^-12). This family is not expected to contribute to the Pareto front."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec requires a shared sin/cos unit for a 16-axis motor-control loop with a 1 MHz tick issuing 32 requests at once, all results back within 0.44 us (p99 batch). This translates to an instantaneous throughput requirement of ~72.7 results/us during the burst, far above the average 32 results/us. The accuracy constraint max_abs_err <= 2^-12 requires at least ~12 CORDIC iterations and sufficient datapath width. Iterative designs cannot meet the burst latency because their cycles-per-result is N+3 >= 15, demanding an impossible Fmax. Unrolled_k with k >= 6 reduces cycles-per-result to ceil(N/k)+3 <= 5 for N >= 12, making the batch bound achievable at ~364 MHz. The exploration therefore focuses almost entirely on unrolled_k, searching k = 4..8 to trade off critical path (Fmax) against cycles-per-result, and N = 10..18, data_width = 12..20, angle_guard = -1..3, frac_guard = 0..3, rounding = trunc/round to find the minimum-area design that meets both accuracy and burst latency. A 5% budget is reserved for iterative to formally confirm infeasibility. The L1 system bound is optimistic, so designs with margin (e.g., batch time well under 0.44 us) are preferred; the search ranges allow Optuna to discover such designs while minimising luts_plus_ffs."
}

{
  "decision": "add_family",
  "next_plan": {
    "families": [
      {
        "budget_share": 0.6,
        "family": "pipelined",
        "ranges": [
          {"choices": ["round", "trunc"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 18, "low": 13, "param": "data_width"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 16, "low": 8, "param": "n_iter"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 3, "low": 0, "param": "angle_guard"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 3, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"}
        ]
      ,
      "why": "only families that emit 1 result/cycle; needed to reach 32 MSPS and to drain a 32-request burst in ~0.44 us"
    },
      {
        "budget_share": 0.4,
        "family": "pipelined_m",
        "ranges": [
          {"choices": ["round", "trunc"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 18, "low": 13, "param": "data_width"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 16, "low": 8, "param": "n_iter"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 3, "low": 0, "param": "angle_guard"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 3, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 8, "low": 2, "param": "m"}
        ]
      ,
      "why": "same 1 result/cycle throughput with fewer registers; cheaper area if Fmax holds"
    }
    ],
    "rationale": "throughput and batch constraints are structurally out of reach for the two explored families"
  },
  "rationale": "Two independent hard walls, both structural. (1) throughput_msps >= 32 is violated by 100% of evals with best 15.3 (iterative) and 12 (unrolled_k); those families spend N+3 or ceil(N/k)+3 cycles per result, so even the fastest observed is ~2x short of 32 MSPS. (2) sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.44 is violated by 100% with best 2.09 (=~21x over): a 32-request-at-once tick cannot be drained by a sub-1-result/cycle datapath, exactly the burst penalty the spec warns about. Both walls are removed only by the 1-result/cycle families (pipelined, pipelined_m), which are unexplored. Accuracy is close but not yet met (best 2^-11.85 vs required 2^-12 at d=15,n=15,guard 2/2), so the pipelined ranges keep data_width 13-18, n_iter 8-16 and both guards 0-3 to find the >=2^-12 region while letting the search trade iterations for area. n_iter is kept low enough that N+2 latency plus 32 drain cycles fits the 0.44 us window. Budget: 40 evals remain."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "Round 2 is complete with the full 100-eval budget spent and it is the final round, so only stop/infeasible are available. Evidence says the explored front is empty and cannot improve: 0 of 100 evaluations feasible, hypervolume 0 (gain +0.0%), and the two binding constraints are violated by every design — throughput_msps best seen 21.1 (unrolled_k) vs 32 required, and sys_p99_batch_us best 1.51 vs 0.44 required. Accuracy is not the blocker at all: 28% of designs already satisfy max_abs_err <= 2.44e-4, with a best of 5.51e-8 (2^-24.11 bits), far better than the 2^-12 floor. Both failing constraints have the same root cause: iterative (best 15.4 MSPS) and unrolled_k (best 21.1 MSPS, even at k=4-8) emit one result every several cycles, so their Fmax x results/cycle ceiling sits well under the 32 requests/us the control tick demands, and the 32-request burst then needs 1.51 us or more to drain versus the 0.44 us p99 batch budget. I am NOT declaring infeasible, because the registry still contains the 1-result/cycle families pipelined and pipelined_m: a pipelined_m design (e.g. m around 8, N around 12, latency ceil(N/m)+2 cycles) at a few tens of MHz would give throughput = Fmax x 1 >= 32 MSPS and drain a 32-request burst in roughly 32 + latency cycles, inside 0.44 us, so feasibility is plausible and unproven either way. Those families were never evaluated (only iterative and unrolled_k were), so the correct honest outcome at the round cap is to stop with an empty front rather than label the spec infeasible on evidence we do not have; the recorded gap is the unexplored 1-result/cycle region, which is where any future run on this spec should start." 
}
